{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "gga-pipe-network",
  "title": "Todini-Pilati GGA in one Math Engine — EPANET Net1 (gravity variant)",
  "category": "Mathematics",
  "description": "The Todini–Pilati Global Gradient Algorithm — the solver at the heart of EPANET — in ONE Math Engine. It was twenty-five matrix and vector nodes and forty-four wires; the algebra is unchanged, but `T`, `inv`, `@`, `pow` and element-wise arithmetic are all operators the engine already has, so each node became one line.\n\nTHE SYSTEM. Unknowns are pipe flows Q (np×1) and junction heads H (nn×1).\n  Energy   A11·Q + A12·H + A10·H0 = 0      with A11 = diag(r|Q|ⁿ⁻¹)\n  Continuity          A21·Q − q = 0        with A21 = A12ᵀ\nA12 is the pipe→junction incidence matrix (−1 at the from-node, +1 at the to-node), A10 the same against fixed-head nodes, H0 the reservoir heads, r the pipe resistances, q the nodal demands.\n\nTHE NEWTON STEP. Eliminating dQ from the Jacobian gives the 'global gradient' form:\n  A  = A21·D11⁻¹·A12          (nn×nn, symmetric positive definite)\n  dH = A⁻¹·[ C − A21·D11⁻¹·E ]\n  dQ = −D11⁻¹·( E + A12·dH )\nwhere E is the energy residual, C the continuity residual and D11 = diag(n·r|Q|ⁿ⁻¹). No diagonal-matrix node is needed: D11⁻¹·A12 is the np×1 column `dinv` broadcast across A12's columns, which is what element-wise multiplication already means.\n\nEVERY INTERMEDIATE IS STILL AN OUTPUT. A11, E, D11⁻¹, A21, A, A⁻¹, C, the RHS, dH and dQ each have their own endpoint and their own row in Output Metrics — the same twenty-five results the twenty-five nodes showed, in one place. Wiring one onward reads exactly as wiring its node did.\n\nTWO THINGS THE NODE CHAIN COULD NOT DO.\n\n  • ANY EXPONENT. The old flow was pinned to n = 2 (Darcy–Weisbach, h = rQ²) because raising a column element-wise needed a node that did not exist. `pow` is in the engine, so `Head-loss exponent n` is now a parameter — 1.852 for Hazen–Williams solves as readily as 2.\n  • MORE THAN ONE STEP. `Newton iterations` repeats the step on its own result. At 1 the outputs are exactly the old flow's, so nothing about the original demonstration changes; raise it and `Q — solved` / `H — solved` carry the converged answer, with the residuals to prove it.\n\nTHE SAMPLE NETWORK is one reservoir at 100 m feeding three junctions through four pipes, demands of 60 and 40 l/s. One step moves the flows to [0.1, 0.0478, 0.0522, −0.0122] m³/s; by eight iterations they settle at [0.1, 0.046445, 0.053555, −0.013555] with heads [99.670573, 99.685273] and a continuity residual of exactly zero. The negative fourth flow is the algorithm telling you that pipe runs the other way.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO. Demand-driven analysis of a single loading, no pressure-dependent demand, no pumps or valves (no A11 augmentation for head-gain elements), no minor losses, and no check that A is non-singular — a network with an unconnected junction returns an empty inverse rather than an error. The step is undamped Newton: a wildly wrong initial Q can overshoot, which is what the iteration count and the residual outputs are there to show.\n\n── THIS COPY CARRIES A REAL NETWORK ──\nThe operands are no longer the four-pipe demonstration: they are EPANET Net1 (gravity variant), 12 pipes and 9 junctions against 1 fixed-head node(s), written out by tools/epanet/inp2canvas.mjs from the EPANET .inp.\n\nORDERING IS THE CONTRACT. Row i of A12, A10, r, Q and every result is pipe `10`, `11`, `12`, `21`, `22`, `31`, `110`, `111`, `112`, `113`, `121`, `122` in that order; column j of A12 and row j of q and H is junction `10`, `11`, `12`, `13`, `21`, `22`, `23`, `31`, `32`. The fixed-head node(s) in H0 are `2`.\n\nr IS SI AND ALREADY CARRIES THE LENGTH: r = 10.667·L / (C^1.852·d^4.871) for Hazen-Williams, so h = r·Q^1.852 with Q in m³/s gives metres. q is m³/s too — a 150 gpm demand is 0.009464, not 9.464.\n\nVERIFIED: 60 iterations bring the energy residual to ~1e-10 and the flows to within 1e-4 L/s of what the official EPANET 2.2 toolkit reports for the same file. See tools/epanet/verify.mjs.\n\nWHAT THIS ROUTE CANNOT DO that the Pipe Network Solver node can: it does not read the canvas. The matrices are typed operands, so the incidence and the resistances must be rebuilt when the drawing changes. Use it to see the algebra; use the solver node to work.",
